syntax: zsh subscripts that follow a subscript

zsh applies a further subscript to the result of the one before it, in
both the braced and the short form, so zsh -n accepts, and zsh expands,

	a=(foo bar)
	echo ${a[2][1]} $a[1][2,3]       # b oo
	if (( ${funcstack[2,-1][(I)_description]} )); then :; fi
	newch=${funcs[1][pos]}

Completion functions use it to pick a character out of an array element or
to search a slice. The parser stopped after the first subscript and failed
with "not a valid parameter expansion operator: `[`".

With LangZsh, the further subscripts of a ParamExp are parsed into Elems,
as the ksh93 variant already does for ${a[i][j]}, and End accounts for them
in the short form too.

Reproducer, before this change: `echo ${a[2][1]}` with LangZsh failed with
"not a valid parameter expansion operator: `[`".

